\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


Spectral independence bounds the operator norm or largest eigenvalue of the source\textquotesingle s conditional influence matrices, uniformly after fixing subsets of coordinates. Mixing is measured by closeness to the stationary law, and $n$ is the number of coordinates.

A Markov chain evolves according to a transition kernel; mixing time measures convergence of its law to the stationary law, usually in total variation. Glauber dynamics updates a single coordinate at a time according to its conditional distribution.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
How rapidly do Markov chains mix under spectral independence, and when can the general polynomial bound be improved to the optimal $O(n\log n)$? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2024/27}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The Markov update rule, influence normalization, stationary distribution class, accuracy, and constants required for the target bound are source-specific.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Sharpen spectral-independence mixing bounds to the stated optimal scale where possible.

